17,61,17,1124,15136,220,20,61,17,198,59,921,59,9640,16,20,15,284,220,17,1124,15136,220,18,1124,15136,220,20,61,17,198,59,2533,785,479,6484,374,279,1985,315,279,15457,2355,315,1817,10250,8168,4185,311,2176,5109,510,59,9640,59,1318,90,38,6484,25547,16,15,15,11,220,16,20,15,8,284,220,17,61,16,1124,15136,220,20,61,17,284,220,20,15,198,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nHow many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150?\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo determine how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we start by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 150.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of 100 and 150:\n\\[\n100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor common to both numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2^1 \\times 5^2 = 50\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2a3bd8d0c6cec2ca48d1c2386dcc3b33dcb66fc3b6d06240f2b5764e1d57d851"},"seconds":21.366501092910767,"seed":23294575141909812,"stop":"eos","text":"To find how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we first need to determine the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 150. This will give us the largest number that divides both 100 and 150.\n\nStarting with the prime factorizations:\n\\[\n100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we identify the common factors and their lowest powers:\n- Both 100 and 150 have the factor \\(2\\) (with the lowest power being \\(2^1\\)).\n- Both 100 and 150 have the factor \\(5^2\\).\n\nTherefore, the GCD of 100 and 150 is:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2^1 \\times 5^2 = 2 \\times 25 = 50\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to identify all the positive two-digit factors of 50. We list the factors of 50:\n\\[\n50 = 1 \\times 50, \\quad 2 \\times 25, \\quad 5 \\times 10\n\\]\n\nFrom these, the only two-digit factor is 50 itself.\n\nThus, the number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"To find how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we first need to determine the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 150. This will give us the largest number that divides both 100 and 150.\n\nStarting with the prime factorizations:\n\\[\n100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we identify the common factors and their lowest powers:\n- Both 100 and 150 have the factor \\(2\\) (with the lowest power being \\(2^1\\)).\n- Both 100 and 150 have the factor \\(5^2\\).\n\nTherefore, the GCD of 100 and 150 is:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2^1 \\times 5^2 = 2 \\times 25 = 50\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to identify all the positive two-digit factors of 50. We list the factors of 50:\n\\[\n50 = 1 \\times 50, \\quad 2 \\times 25, \\quad 5 \\times 10\n\\]\n\nFrom these, the only two-digit factor is 50 itself.\n\nThus, the number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]","id":"prealgebra:567"}
